<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/7th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ridda Wars</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/632">632</a>–<a href="/years/633">633 CE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ridda Wars, or Wars of Apostasy, were a series of campaigns fought in 632–633 CE by the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>, Abu Bakr, against Arabian tribes that broke away after the death of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophet-muhammad">Prophet Muhammad</a>. Many tribes rallied around rival prophetic claimants such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musaylima">Musaylima</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sajah">Sajah</a>. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate</a> describe how Abu Bakr divided his forces into eleven corps and reunified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, preparing the ground for the great conquests.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ridda-wars.jpg" alt="Ridda Wars" width="320" height="271" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mohammad Adil / Mohammad adil at en.wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mohammad_adil-Riddah_wars.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In May 632 Muhammad ordered an expedition against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> to avenge those killed at the Battle of Mu'tah, and named Usama ibn Zayd to command it. Muhammad died in June 632 and Abu Bakr was chosen as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saqifah">Saqifah</a>. Rebellions were rising, and many regions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> were withholding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zakat">zakat</a> and leaving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. Usama's army was still to march, and Usama sent Umar to Abu Bakr to ask that it stay at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>, since none would be left to defend the city. A detailed reconstruction of the events is made difficult by contradictory and partisan accounts in the primary sources.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In July 632 Abu Bakr sent envoys to the rebel tribes calling on them to stay loyal and pay <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zakat">zakat</a>, and they refused. Tulayha, whom many tribes also acknowledged as a prophet, gathered an army at Zhu Qissa, about thirty miles east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>. Abu Bakr formed guard units under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-ibn-abi-talib">Ali ibn Abi Talib</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talha-ibn-ubayd-allah">Talha ibn Ubayd Allah</a> and Zubayr ibn al-Awwam, which intercepted the rebel force at night until it retreated.</p><p>After Usama's army returned on 4 August, Abu Bakr moved to Zhu Qissa and fought at Abraq. He then divided the available men into eleven corps, each with its own commander and standard. The strongest, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalid-ibn-al-walid">Khalid ibn al-Walid</a>, moved against Tulayha at Buzakha, while others were sent later according to Khalid's progress.</p><p>Abu Bakr planned to clear west-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> first, then deal with Malik bin Nuwaira, and then with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musaylima">Musaylima</a>, leader of the Banu Hanifa and a rival claimant to prophethood at Yamamah. Hudaifa bin Mihsan and Ikrimah ibn Abi Jahl defeated Laqit bin Malik, called Dhu'l-Taj, at Dibba in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a> in November 632, and Laqit was killed. Musaylima was killed at the Battle of Yamamah, where he had an army of allegedly 40,000.</p><p>Ala bin Al Hadhrami took Hajr in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a> by a night attack, finishing at about the end of January 633. The last great revolt, by the Kinda of Hadhramaut under al-Ash'ath ibn <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qays">Qays</a>, began in January 633 and ended with the siege of the fortress of al-Nujayr, where the besieged fighters were executed and al-Ash'ath was spared.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The campaigns ended in June 633 with all the tribes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> under Abu Bakr. The wars established <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalid-ibn-al-walid">Khalid ibn al-Walid</a>'s reputation as a tactician and cavalry commander. It is unclear whether Abu Bakr then intended a full expansion or pre-emptive raids to make a buffer against the Sasanian and Byzantine empires. Khalid was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> with 18,000 volunteers, and Abu Bakr then sent armies against Roman Syria.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ridda Wars” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Much of Arabia rose in the <b>Ridda Wars</b>, the wars of “apostasy” and secession, as tribes that had submitted to Muhammad treated his death as ending their obligations and rallied around rival prophetic claimants such as Musaylima in Yamama, Tulayha among the Banu Asad and Sajah of the…</blockquote><a href="/empire/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
